        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CFM, MPH and Newtons: Which Number Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            CFM, cubic feet per minute, is the volume of air leaving the tube. It decides how wide a swath you can move at once and how big a pile you can push. MPH is how fast that air leaves the nozzle, which decides how well it lifts leaves stuck to wet grass or wedged against a fence. Neither number alone tells you much, because a narrow nozzle can raise MPH while cutting CFM.
          </p>

          <p>
            Newtons of blowing force combine the two into a single push figure, and where a maker publishes it, it is the most useful comparison. On our list that ranges from about 20 newtons on the Ryobi battery backpack, as Pro Tool Reviews measured it, to 41 on the Stihl BR 800 and 44 to 48 on the Echo PB-9010T. A handheld typically lands in the teens, which is why the step up feels so different on wet leaves.
          </p>

