          <p>
            Read the CFM figures with care. Husqvarna's own page shows the 350BT at 494 CFM at the nozzle and 692 CFM in the housing, and many retailers advertise the higher number. Stihl publishes figures with and without the tube. When you compare, use nozzle figures where you can find them, and be suspicious of any backpack under 60cc claiming 700 CFM.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, a backpack in the 500 to 700 CFM range at 180 mph or more is plenty. Go past 900 CFM only when you have acreage or debris that genuinely needs it, because the extra power arrives with extra weight, fuel and noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-vs-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gas or Battery
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas still wins on sustained force. The big two-stroke and 4-MIX backpacks on this list push more newtons for longer, and a refill takes a minute. If you clear wet leaves on an acre or more every weekend in fall, gas remains the practical choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery backpacks have closed the gap on airflow but not on runtime at full power. EGO rates the LB6000 at up to 600 CFM and Ryobi rates the RY40440 at 625 CFM, both respectable figures, but both drop to around 145 mph and both drain fast on turbo. Pro Tool Reviews reported about 23 minutes on high for the Ryobi with one 5.0Ah pack, and EGO's UK listing gives 15 minutes on max with its smaller battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            The battery case is strongest for medium yards, dry leaves, noise-sensitive neighborhoods and anyone who hates engine maintenance. No mixing fuel, no stale-gas carburetor problems in spring, no pull cord and much less noise. If you already own a 56V EGO or 40V Ryobi mower, the batteries cross over, which takes much of the sting out of the price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget honestly for a second battery if you choose cordless. Most people who are disappointed by battery backpacks bought one pack and expected gas-length sessions on turbo. Two packs, used on the normal setting with turbo saved for stubborn spots, solve that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-and-fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Weight and Harness Fit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Listed weights on our picks run from about 21 to 27 pounds, and that range matters more than it looks after an hour. The Stihl BR 600 is notably light for its power at 21.6 pounds, the Echo PB-580T and Husqvarna 350BT sit around 22.5 to 23 pounds, and the 80cc machines land between 24.5 and 26.7 depending on whose figure you believe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check whether the weight is dry or fueled. A full tank adds several pounds, and the big Echo's roughly 84-ounce tank is heavy when full. Battery weights depend on the pack, and Ryobi lists its kit, battery included, at 21.1 pounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Harness design decides comfort as much as weight. Look for padded shoulder straps, a back pad that keeps engine heat off you, and ideally a hip belt that moves load onto your hips. Pro Tool Reviews flagged the lack of a hip strap on the Ryobi as a weakness. Adjust the straps so the machine sits high and close to your back rather than hanging low.
          </p>

          <p>
            Throttle position is a fit question too. A tube-mounted throttle, as on the Echo PB-580T and Husqvarna 350BT, keeps speed control in the hand that aims the nozzle. Hip-mounted throttles suit long commercial sessions but can feel awkward to occasional users.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Noise and Your Neighbors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Backpack blowers are loud, and the noise figures are hard to compare because brands measure differently. Most US makers quote decibels at 50 feet under ANSI B175.2, which is the number many local ordinances use. Husqvarna publishes the sound at the operator's ear and the total sound power instead, which produce much bigger numbers that are not directly comparable.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the 50-foot basis, listings give about 70 dB for the Echo PB-580T, 75 dB(A) for the Stihl BR 600, 77 to 78 for the BR 800 and 80 to 82 for the Echo PB-9010T. Ryobi rates its battery backpack at 59 dB. Each 10 dB step sounds roughly twice as loud, so the gap between the quiet and loud ends of this list is very noticeable to neighbors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your local rules before buying. A growing number of towns limit blower hours or decibel levels, and some restrict gas blowers outright. If you live in one, the battery backpacks are not just the polite choice but the legal one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect your own hearing regardless. Even a 70 dB-at-50-feet blower is far louder at the operator's position, which is why Husqvarna's operator-ear figure is in the 90s. Wear hearing protection and eye protection with every backpack on this list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance That Keeps a Backpack Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas backpacks need the same care as any small two-stroke engine. Use fresh fuel mixed at the ratio in the manual, ideally with a stabilizer, and do not let mixed fuel sit for months. Stale fuel is the most common reason a blower will not start in spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean or replace the air filter regularly, because blowers live in dust. Check the spark plug each season. Stihl's 4-MIX engine on the BR 600 and BR 800 also needs periodic valve clearance checks, which is a dealer job for most owners.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the end of the season, either run the tank dry or add stabilizer and run the engine to get treated fuel into the carburetor. Our <a href="/how-to-clean-gutters-before-winter/">fall gutter cleaning guide</a> is a good reminder to do this when you put the blower away after the last leaf cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery backpacks need far less. Keep the vents and intake clear, store the batteries indoors at moderate temperature and partial charge over winter, and check the tubes and straps for wear. That simplicity is a large part of their appeal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What a Backpack Blower Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices move with promotions and dealers, so we have not quoted list prices, but the tiers are consistent. Budget gas backpacks like the Husqvarna 350BT sit at the bottom, mid-size homeowner models like the Echo PB-580T in the middle, and commercial Magnums and 80cc Echos at the top, often costing two to three times the entry models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery backpacks look competitive until you price batteries. The EGO LB6000 is sold without a battery, and a large EGO pack is a significant cost on its own. The Ryobi kit includes a battery and charger, but serious users will want a second. If you already own the platform, the math changes in your favor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs favor battery. Gas machines burn mixed fuel and two-stroke oil, need plugs, filters and occasional carburetor work, and benefit from dealer service. Battery machines cost a little electricity per charge but eventually need replacement packs, which are the big long-term expense.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest blower over five years is the one sized correctly. An 80cc Magnum on a half-acre lot wastes fuel and your back; an underpowered unit on two acres wastes hours. Match the machine to the property and the debris before you look at price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Backpack Blower to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical large suburban yard, half an acre to an acre with some trees, the Echo PB-580T is the pick. It moves enough air for wet leaves at a weight most people can carry for an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            For an acre or more of mature trees, step up to the Stihl BR 600 Magnum, which pairs commercial airflow with a relatively light 21.6 pounds. Choose the Echo PB-9010T or Stihl BR 800 C-E only when you have big acreage or heavy debris that truly uses 900-plus CFM.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to avoid gas, choose by platform. EGO owners should look at the LB6000, and Ryobi 40V owners at the RY40440, which is also the pick for noise-sensitive areas. Either way, buy a second battery for big jobs. The same airflow-versus-resistance logic applies indoors too; our <a href="/best-dust-collector/">dust collector guide</a> explains why rated CFM rarely matches what you get at the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you only need a backpack occasionally, the Husqvarna 350BT is a reasonable budget entry, provided you compare it on its real 494 CFM nozzle figure rather than the 692 CFM on many listings.
          </p>
        </div>

